2026, day 105

Good morning from Asia.

‘Strait of Hormuz tankers stop or turn around amid US blockade’ (FT) - “US military says no ships pass through its operation to seal off Iranian ports in first 24 hours”;

‘U.S. Says No Ships Passed Iran Blockade in First 24 Hours’ (WSJ);

‘Strait of Hormuz traffic barely affected on first day of US blockade, data shows’ (Reuters) - “three Iran-linked vessels that transited the strait were not heading to Iranian ports and were not affected”;

‘Sanctioned Chinese tanker U-turns after passing through Hormuz’ (Telegraph) - “Vessel carrying 250,000 barrels of methanol was on course to be first to head through key oil passage during US blockade”;

‘Saudi Arabia pressures Trump to scale back war on Iran’ (Telegraph) - “fearing Iran could retaliate by blockading the Red Sea”;

‘Is the US trying to pressure China with Hormuz blockade?’ (SCMP) - “

‘US Treasury says not renewing temporary Iran oil sanction easing’ (AFP);

‘Trump says US-Iran talks could resume in Pakistan over next two days’ (Reuters);

‘US inadvertently handed Iran’s leadership a safe place to regroup’ (Telegraph) - “Talks in Pakistan gave the Iranians somewhere to meet without fear of being targeted by air strikes”;

‘Vice President Vance says US-Iran mistrust cannot be solved overnight’ (Reuters);

‘Vance stresses Trump wants ‘grand bargain’ with Iran, not ‘a small deal’’ (ToI);

‘Iran Can Thrive if It Commits to Not Having Nuclear Weapon, Vance Says’ (WSJ);

‘Removal of enriched uranium 'threshold condition' for ending Iran campaign, Katz says’ (JPost);

‘Europe Drafts Postwar Plan to Free Up Hormuz Without U.S.’ (WSJ);

‘Top Russian and Chinese envoys meet in Beijing to discuss Iran, Ukraine and Taiwan’ (SCMP);

‘Mossad chief: Our mission isn’t over until regime falls’ (ToI);

‘Israeli, Lebanese envoys hold historic summit in Washington to discuss peace deal’ (ToI);

‘Israeli ambassador: Lebanon wants to be liberated from Hezbollah’ (Al Jazeera) - “We discovered today that we’re on the same side of the equation.”;

‘Ukraine asks Israel to detain Russian ship carrying stolen grain that docked in Haifa’ (ToI);

‘Donald Trump says Giorgia Meloni lacks courage’ (FT);

‘Europe Is Accelerating a NATO Fallback Plan in Case Trump Pulls Out’ (WSJ) - “The Continent is drawing up a contingency for greater European involvement as tensions rise over Iran war”;

‘France scales down participation to Balikatan exercises in the Philippines’ (Naval News);

‘Fear of populists is pushing governments to oppose expanding the EU’ (Politico);

‘Serbia agrees deal to produce combat drones with Israel’ (AP);

‘U.S. eases bank sanctions amid Venezuela's economic woes’ (Axios);

‘U.S. suggests Cuba complicit in helping Russia fight Ukraine’ (Axios);

In geoeconomics, ‘Iran war escalation could trigger global recession, IMF warns’ (Guardian);

‘Brussels pitches state subsidy bonanza to combat Iran war energy shock’ (Euractiv) - “The draft EU plan “goes much further than the current state aid rules”, said one expert”;

‘Canada to suspend federal taxes on gasoline and diesel’ (Al Jazeera);

‘Chalmers’ retrospective tax grab shocks investors’ (AFR) - “Treasurer Jim Chalmers has shocked foreign investors in local mining, energy and infrastructure by imposing a retrospective capital gains tax dating back 20 years”;

‘China shock 2.0: the flood of high-tech goods that will change the world’ (FT);

‘China's Control Over Tech Threatens India’s Manufacturing Dreams’ (BBG);

‘China snaps up US chip tools via Southeast Asia amid supply chain shift’ (Nikkei Asia);

‘Bessent Says Trump’s Tariff Rates Could Be Restored by July’ (BBG);

‘Richer EU states gain upper hand in €400bn competitiveness fund battle’ (Euractiv) - “A draft EU budget plan prioritises “excellence” in allocating competitiveness cash”;

In markets, ‘Kevin Warsh is very rich, or possibly very, very rich’ (FT);

‘Nasdaq Logs Longest Winning Streak Since 2021 as Investors Look Beyond War’ (WSJ)
